Looking at this problem, I need to prove that under the given condition, there exists a transversal (hitting set) of size at most 2003.

**Proof.**

Assume the condition holds: for every 2004 societies, some person belongs to at least 11 of them.

Consider a **maximal** collection $\mathcal{F}$ of societies such that every person belongs to at most 10 societies in $\mathcal{F}$. (Such a collection exists since the empty collection satisfies the degree constraint, and we extend greedily until no further society can be added.)

**Claim 1:** $|\mathcal{F}| \le 2003$.

If $|\mathcal{F}| \ge 2004$, take any 2004 societies from $\mathcal{F}$. By construction, every person belongs to at most 10 of them, contradicting the hypothesis that some person belongs to at least 11. $\square$

Let $D = \{$people belonging to exactly 10 societies in $\mathcal{F}\}$.

**Claim 2:** Every society not in $\mathcal{F}$ contains a member of $D$.

By maximality, adding any society $S \notin \mathcal{F}$ to $\mathcal{F}$ violates the degree constraint, so some member of $S$ already has degree 10 in $\mathcal{F}$, i.e., belongs to $D$. $\square$

Let $\mathcal{F}' = \{$societies in $\mathcal{F}$ with no member in $D\}$. These are exactly the societies in $\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members have degree $\le 9$ in $\mathcal{F}$.

For each society in $\mathcal{F}'$, pick one member and let $R$ be the set of all picked members, so $|R| \le |\mathcal{F}'|$.

Set $T = D \cup R$. Then $T$ is a transversal:
- Every society outside $\mathcal{F}$ contains a member of $D \subseteq T$ (Claim 2).
- Every society in $\mathcal{F} \setminus \mathcal{F}'$ contains a member of $D \subseteq T$ (by definition of $\mathcal{F}'$).
- Every society in $\mathcal{F}'$ is hit by $R \subseteq T$.

**Claim 3:** $|D| + |\mathcal{F}'| \le |\mathcal{F}|$.

Each person in $D$ belongs to exactly 10 societies in $\mathcal{F}$, and every such society contains a degree-10 member, hence lies in $\mathcal{F} \setminus \mathcal{F}'$. This gives $10|D|$ incidences between $D$ and $\mathcal{F} \setminus \mathcal{F}'$. Each society in $\mathcal{F} \setminus \mathcal{F}'$ has exactly 10 members, at most 10 of whom lie in $D$, so it accounts for at most 10 such incidences. Therefore
$$10|D| \le 10\,|\mathcal{F} \setminus \mathcal{F}'| = 10\,(|\mathcal{F}| - |\mathcal{F}'|),$$
giving $|D| \le |\mathcal{F}| - |\mathcal{F}'|$, i.e., $|D| + |\mathcal{F}'| \le |\mathcal{F}|$. $\square$

**Conclusion:**
$$|T| \le |D| + |R| \le |D| + |\mathcal{F}'| \le |\mathcal{F}| \le 2003.$$

So the government can arrest the at most 2003 people in $T$, and every society has at least one arrested member. $\blacksquare$

